From: William Wiser <sbrooks@chaph.usc.edu>
Date: Wed, 19 Oct 1994 16:24:30 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-348] PSYCH: Self-Esteem?

Romana wrote:
> A more radical individualist might say something like, "I feel good
> about myself because I am me", and be unconcerned with self-transformation
> or personal power.
  
Or perhaps "I feel good about myself because I am the most valuable and
interesting being in my universe," and be concerned with self transformation
because they are an unfinished work of art and want to increase their
ability to bring themselves pleasure.
 
That's not quite my attitude (I'm getting too old to have any one attitude),
but it does have a certain charm.

<Humble on> I find myself in awe of an existence that could create a being as
wonderful as me. I would like to explore further and see just how
fascinating I can become and what other wonders exist in the universe around
me. <Humble off>.
 
Self-esteem is a mix of thoughts and chemicals and ultimately comes from an
understanding of your capabilities and the rules of the game (plus a little
prozac for some of us). The best measure I can think of at the moment is
to ask the question "Am I having fun yet?"
 
Self acceptance on the other hand, sounds like a subcategory of acceptance and
has to do with not arguing with reality. "I am what I am." Both self-esteem
and self acceptance are important but I see no reason to expect less than
total mastery in the game of life since failing would hardly bother me if I
were dead or hopelessly out of the game.
 
