From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Wed, 19 Oct 1994 21:11:49 -0700
Subject: [#94-10-352] Tolerance & Astrology

Why does Minsky think that if his students are unwilling to totally condemn
astrology as totally worthless that this means there's something wrong with
them?  That's a non-sequitur.  Even if astrology can only get good results by
telling you stuff that's true only by accident, that doesn't mean that it's
worthless, only that it's of less value that something that can get good
results more reliably.  Or better results by mere accident, for that matter.
Methinks he's asking too much of his students.

The way I look at it is that astrology is a sort of spontaneous order, part
of what we've inherited from the culture of ours which has been evolving for
the past 6,000 years or more.  If there's a tradition that old which has
been trying to come up with ways of predicting things about people, then it's
only natural to expect that it'll have come up with something.  It may not
conform to contemporary concepts of scientific method and thus be inferior to
contemporary science, but that hardly means it's entirely worthless!  The
ancient Egyptians and Mesopotamians didn't have any scientifically sound
mathematics, either, but they did pretty good when it came to their rudimentary
geometry and architecture.  Who would condemn their numerical practices as
totally worthless?

When it comes to the question of tolerance, it's very important to distinguish
between the "judge not, lest you be judged yourself" or "judge not, lest ye
hurt someone's feelings" theories of tolerance and simple respect for the
fact that everyone has to make up their own mind about things based upon facts,
logic, and reliable authorities as best they can.

